{
  "dom_id": "question-167568",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "The acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}\u2061t$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=2{csc}^{2}\u2061(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=4u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t) \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t) \\\\  & =-4{csc}^{2}\u2061t\\operatorname{cot}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
